1.184.m:
where the blossoms of mango trees, upon which bees had settled, / appeared like arrows inscribed with love's name, belonging to Kāma, striking embodied beings;
where passionate women caused their lovers entirely to abandon restraint, / and courtesy was displayed everywhere by the southern wind;
bearing flowering barley plants stirred by the gentle breezes, / the rows of fields shone like flowing rivers;
the lines of woods, embraced on all sides by spring, / openly displayed their passion through the blooming palāśa trees;
the earth, strewn with flowers and leaves by the gentle breeze and covered in grass, / bore the splendor of a cloudless night sky;
wherein, O best of Gandharvas, great beauty reigned in the cow-pens / because of the frolics of newborn, softly-lowing calves;
in which, at the end of day, the cows entered, / lowing loudly and hastening their steps, eager for their calves;
where the cowherds felt supreme joy again and again / as the calves with uplifted tails drank from their mothers' udders and were licked by them;

where the shoot-like hands of passionate women gathered flowers / and, owing to their resemblance to fresh shoots, could not even be distinguished by their lovers;
where, hearing the lute-melody of humming bees in the concert of spring cuckoos, / the creepers shaken by the gentle breeze seemed to dance.
When that spring had passed, O king, summer scorched the entire world, / like Lavaṇa himself, blocking paths, with immense foliage.

1.185.m:
1.241
Nāḍāyana said:
Then, when summer arrived, full of blooming jasmine, / when travelers' journeys ceased and heaps of sand grew burning hot;

the lovely rows of forests adorned with dhārākadamba, bakula, kuṭaja, and arjuna trees, / abounding in ripe rose-apples and mangoes;
in which the earth, scorched by the heat of summer and sprinkled with fresh water, / gives off its own characteristic fragrance and garland-like quality;
while the clouds thunder like the sound of mṛdaṅga drums, / in this [season] peacocks with expanding, splendid tails dance;
clouds are impelled in the sky by a gentle breeze, / and female buffaloes wander about with udders full of milk;
at dawn, as the buffaloes roam together, / the sweet ringing of their bells is heard in the thickets of grass;
in which the high grounds, as if with unblinking eyes through herds of black antelope, / look upon the earth filled with water, O Śatrughna;
by the lovely downpour of clouds glistening with multitudes of raindrops, / passionate women are made to abandon [spite] together with their lovers, O descendant of Raghu;
rivers become impassable; kings abandon warfare; / the sky, canopied by hosts of clouds, is likewise engaged in conflict;

1.245
Rāma said:
When the lotus-eyed Lord has awakened, in winter, the season sacred to Viṣṇu, / you should carry out the layout of the city or of the countryside:
A season delightful with ripe oranges, its rivers shrouded in fog, / its fields of grain reaped, and its bees slender-bellied;
At a time when provisions have been gathered, when kings set out for war, / when the earth is free of mud, and the reservoirs are charming with sweet, cold water;
Where the nights are long, exceedingly fit for lovemaking, / deeply enchanting to the young, and inviting tight embraces;
Where, on the pretext of the cold, even proud women, / relinquishing their injured pride, themselves celebrate embracing their lover’s neck;
A time when the water of pleasing mountain springs is comfortably warm, / when winds have grown strong, and the ground has turned dark green;
When the riverbanks are hard to discern, concealed by rising mists, / and known only by the cries of Indian cranes; a time dear to lovers;
Where the poles of sugarcane presses, turned by men, / rise and fall like the fortunes of mortals;

Where the twice-born perform the worship of the first fruits and offer oblations, / and where farmers rejoice at bringing in the harvest;
Where, in houses comfortably warmed by the breath of cattle, / the sound of the brahmins chanting the sacred texts brings happiness to men;
Where refined sugar is boiled and raw sugar is prepared; / a delightful, very cold season when fire is dear to living beings;
Where wealthy men of attractive appearance walk about, / wrapped in heavy cloaks, their limbs anointed with saffron;
At a time fit for riding horses, when the sun's rays are mild, / hunting is beloved, my child, especially to the young;
Where the buttermilk of buffalo cows, nourished on red grass, / is pleasantly scented with orange, endowed with fragrance, color, and taste;
Where sugarcanes, vidārī tubers, and radishes, / as well as delicacies made of flour, are especially flavorful;
When people begin rituals such as investiture with the sacred girdle and marriages, / when the lotus-eyed Lord has awakened, and sacrificial preparations are undertaken;

1.186.m:
Those cows are proclaimed to be ten, observing the vow of celibacy.
Hear from me their mind-born offspring in their respective order:
cows, female buffaloes, female goats, and female sheep.
Gandharvī gave birth to horses with the speed of wind.
Garuḍī and Mayūrī were two delightful daughters;
they were given by Pulaha to the great-souled Garuḍa.
Garuḍī gave birth to eagles of terrible prowess;
Mayūrī gave birth to peacocks and other birds.
Born in the lineage of Garuḍa, all are devourers of snakes.
Birds are to be known as flying creatures; hear now of the eagles, the garuḍas:
Suvarṇacūḍa, Nāga, Dāruṇa, Cūḍatuṇḍaka,
Anila, Anala, Viśālākṣa, and Kuṇḍalī;
Karkaśa, Dhvajaviṣkambhī, Vinata, and Vāmana,
Vātavega, Diśācakṣus, Nimiṣa, and Animiṣa;
Trivāra, Saptavāra, Vālmīki, and Vipuki,
Daityadvīpa, Sariddvīpa, Sārasa, and Padmaketana;

And Varuṇa in sovereignty over the waters, as well as over the aquatic creatures; / and Kubera, the eldest son of Viśravas who has man as his mount, [in sovereignty] over the yakṣas, rākṣasas, kings, and wealth;
[and he consecrated] the mighty Vipracitti as king over the dānavas;
and the Mountain Lord who holds the trident in his hand [as lord] of all bhūtas and piśācas; / and Himavat of mountains, and the salt [ocean] of rivers;
and Bhārata of all realms, and the central one of continents; / the fresh-water [ocean] of oceans, and the aśvattha of all branched trees;
and resolve of actions, and Meru of all rock-bearing mountains; / Citraratha of the gandharvas, and Garuḍa of birds;
Uccaiḥśravas of horses, and the breeding bull of cattle; / gold of all gems, and barley of herbs;
and Satyaloka of worlds, and Rasātala of the nether worlds; / and the Yajurveda of all authoritative scriptures without distinction;
and the Gāyatrī of all mantras and meters, O best of the Yadus; / and the tiger of beasts, and Takṣaka of serpents;

Airāvata of lordly elephants, and dharma of all happiness; / Vāsuki of all nāgas, and Bhṛgu of the sages;
and the goddess Sarasvatī of all vidyādharas; / and Rāhu of portents, and the sun of heavenly bodies;
and the year as the lord over time and the divisions of time; / and fever in sovereignty over diseases; and the sun and moon over mortals.
Indeed, O king of kings, he was consecrated by Brahmā himself / in this realm of Bhārata in the past, when the Vaivasvata period arrived.
Therefore, even today, kings are born from portions of those two. / Then Lord Śatakratu, the mighty ruler of the three worlds, / was consecrated in sovereignty over the gods, having assembled with all the kings.
Vajra said: / How was that great-souled slayer of the enemies of the gods consecrated / in sovereignty, having assembled with all of them? Tell me this, foremost of the Bhṛgus; this devotion of mine is to the foe of the enemies of the gods.

1.187.m:
Śailūṣa said:
I wish to hear at length the origin of elephants;
for elephants, O highly fortunate one, are exceedingly dear to me.

one tossed again and again, making her bed dishevelled; / one lay upon a bed of lotus fibres, on a couch of plantain leaves;
one grew angry at sandalwood, at moonbeams, and at lotus-stalk bracelets / that were powerless to extinguish the fire of love;
streams of tears, falling upon the bosom of a certain woman inflamed by love's fire, / vanished like [water drops] upon a frying pan;
one woman fainted upon her bed as she described to her female friends / the joy born of union with her beloved, which she had obtained then by chance in sleep;
one, fanned by her female friends with cool breezes from palm-leaf fans, / was burned all the more by [the hot air] issuing from her own sighs;
"My friend, sleep, which would bring about union with my beloved even in a dream, / has by fate been destroyed for me," said yet another;
one woman, her body wasted by love, whose splendid armlet ornament / had slipped to the place of her bracelet, became an object of pity, O friend;
another, grieved over by her female companions because her meditation was fixed on her beloved, / did not speak to her friends, being unaware [of them];
